Root cause:
The bug arises from the improper timing of the `nvme_stop_keep_alive()` function call, which was originally moved to a position that could lead to keep-alive commands being sent after all I/O operations had been terminated. This could result in unexpected behavior or errors, as keep-alive commands should not be issued when the controller is no longer processing I/O requests.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
nvme_stop_keep_alive(ctrl);
```
```c
if (ctrl->admin_tagset) {
```
```c
blk_mq_tagset_wait_completed_request(ctrl->admin_tagset);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because `nvme_stop_keep_alive()` was called before ensuring that all I/O operations had been terminated. This improper timing could lead to keep-alive commands being sent even after the controller had stopped processing I/O requests, which is not allowed and can cause unexpected behavior or errors.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
nvme_stop_keep_alive(ctrl);
```
```c
nvm_stop_ctrl(struct nvme_ctrl *ctrl)
```
Reason:
The fix involves moving the call to `nvme_stop_keep_alive(ctrl)` back to the `nvme_stop_ctrl()` function, ensuring that it is executed after all I/O operations have been properly terminated. This guarantees that no keep-alive commands are sent when the controller is no longer processing I/O, thus preventing the potential for errors or unexpected behavior.


